  "story": [
   "Walk towards the big red engine in the middle of the hall, and stop where you can see all of it. This is the Burnley Mill Engine, built in 1903 by the Burnley Ironworks Company. Its job was to drive the looms of a cotton mill in Burnley, in Lancashire: well over a thousand looms, all running off this one engine. The weavers made cloth that was sold all round the world. Now imagine the noise of a thousand looms going at once, and be grateful the engine came without them.",
   "Walk round to the flywheel end, where the great wheel is. This engine was still at work in the mill in 1970. Hold on to that date. In 1969, people walked on the Moon. So for a while the space age and the steam age were running side by side, and one of them was in a Lancashire engine room. Apollo 10, the dress rehearsal for that Moon landing, is on this floor too, at the back of the building. You will get there. Every now and then, with no timetable at all, this engine runs. If it does, you will know. It is loud.",
   "Now find the steam turbine, built by the firm of Charles Parsons. It looks modest next to its neighbours. Do not be fooled. The museum puts it plainly: steam has been the driving force behind British industry for three hundred years, and without it the Industrial Revolution could never have happened. Parsons built his first turbine in 1884. Instead of pushing a piston back and forth, the steam spins a wheel of blades. The museum's own page says steam still makes most of the electricity we use. That was true for most of the last century. Britain stopped burning coal for power in 2024, and the wind now does a big share of the work, but steam turbines still turn in every nuclear power station in the country. So the oldest idea in the room is still helping to keep the lights on.",
   "Now look for an older kind of engine altogether: the atmospheric engine, the type that came before James Watt. The first were built by Thomas Newcomen, from 1712. Find the Pentrich engine. It is an atmospheric engine, the kind Newcomen built, and it is thought to date from 1791, long after Newcomen and after Watt had started improving the design. Old ideas take a while to retire. Then find the small model of a separate condenser, from about 1765. That was Watt's big idea, and probably the most important of his improvements. So the man everyone credits with the steam engine was really its improver.",
   "Now find Old Bess. Look for her label. She is thought to have been built by Boulton and Watt in 1777, and to have worked until 1848, which would make her the oldest surviving Watt engine. Her first runs were said to be so violent that she was nicknamed Beelzebub, and Matthew Boulton himself called her action very fierce. Then, the story goes, in July 1778 her engine house caught fire, and the roof burned down in half an hour. She was rebuilt. A devil's name, a house burned down around her, and she is the one that survived. Never judge an engine by its first week.",
   "Next, the Lap Engine, from 1788. The museum calls it the oldest essentially unaltered rotative engine in the world. Rotative means it turned a wheel, rather than simply pumping up and down, and it incorporates all of Watt's most important steam engine improvements. At Matthew Boulton's Soho Manufactory in Birmingham it drove forty-three metal polishing machines, known as lapping machines, which is presumably where the name comes from. It did that for seventy years. Seventy years of polishing other things, and it is the one that has come out of history looking shiny.",
   "Last, turn towards the north side of the hall, where the lifts are in the corner. Over there, behind glass, is James Watt's own attic workshop. Do not go in yet. That is where this walk ends. When you come back down by the lifts in that corner, the workshop will be waiting, and by then you will know a good deal more about the world his engines made. For now, head west, along the corridor, towards the back of the building. The engines came first. Everything else in this museum came after."
  ],
